        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Laozi</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">traditionally 6th century BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">In the village of Quren in the state of Chu, present-day Luyi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, as the traditional accounts have it, in the 6th century BCE; the son of the Censor-in-Chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> and Lady Yishou</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">Not recorded; in the best-known account he departed west through a frontier gate at the age of 80, and some religious traditions hold that he became an immortal hermit</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Keeper of the Archives for the royal Zhou court in the tradition; regarded by Taoists as the founder of the faith and, in some sects, as a god; historians treat him as a figure of doubtful historicity</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daodejing">Tao Te Ching</a> (Dào Dé Jīng); the idea of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-wei">wu wei</a>; the encounter with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>; the departure to the west; the ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>'s rulers; the founding figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a></span></div>
