    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Laozi, meaning 'Old Master', is an honorific and not a personal name; the traditional accounts name him Li Er and describe him as a keeper of the archives at the Zhou court.</li><li>By the mid-twentieth century most Western scholars regarded his historicity as doubtful and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daodejing">Tao Te Ching</a> as a compilation by many hands.</li><li>The book teaches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tao">Tao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-wei">wu wei</a>, and Laozi became a god in later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a> and an imperial ancestor under the Tang.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Taoist myths say that Laozi was conceived when his mother gazed upon a falling star, remained in her womb for 62 years and was born as a grown man with a full grey beard while she leaned against a plum tree.</p></aside>
    </section>

